// The demo's subject vehicle, as an ENVIRONMENT ACTOR rather than a spawned
// object.
//
// Why this exists
// ---------------
// The car used to be moved by client-side teleport, one set_object_pose per
// control tick. The motion model was fine - moving_car.py brakes into corners
// under a lateral acceleration limit and accelerates out under a longitudinal
// one - but it was being SAMPLED at the control loop's rate. Measured: 8.69 Hz
// while moving, 54 cm steps, against a recorder capturing at 15.57 Hz, so 44 %
// of frames during motion showed a position already shown. That is what read as
// choppy.
//
// An environment actor takes a whole trajectory once, through
// World.import_ned_trajectory() + EnvActor.set_trajectory(), and the SIMULATOR
// interpolates it at its own render rate. The judder goes away by construction
// rather than by out-running it, and it costs no per-frame RPC - which matters,
// because scene motion and the detector share the simulator's RPC budget and
// det_hz only just cleared its 4.0 Hz gate.
//
// A thread was tried first and rejected: the Project AirSim client is not
// thread-safe, and driving poses from a second thread aborted the mission with
// a pynng ConnectionReset. See
// docs/FINDING-scene-motion-belongs-to-the-simulator.md.
//
// Links must reference an unreal_mesh by asset path, so the packaged rover body
// is used rather than a glTF - spawn_object_from_file has no equivalent here.
// The path follows the /PluginName/PathUnderContent convention: this mesh is
// PASBlocks/Plugins/Rover/Content/OffroadCar/SM_Offroad_Body.uasset.
{
  "links": [
    {
      "name": "Body",
      "collision": {
        // The aircraft must never be stopped by the car it is following. The
        // Shield keeps separation through the policy, which is where that
        // decision belongs, and a physics collision here would instead show up
        // as an unexplained flight abort.
        "enabled": false
      },
      "visual": {
        "geometry": {
          "type": "unreal_mesh",
          "name": "/Rover/OffroadCar/SM_Offroad_Body"
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "joints": []
  // No "trajectory" block. The route is computed in Python from the same
  // _Path model the old teleporting car used - rounded corners, cornering
  // speed capped by lateral acceleration, braking and accelerating under a
  // longitudinal limit - and uploaded at flight start. Baking a fixed path in
  // here would freeze the route that --route and --car-speed are meant to vary.
}
